Subject: [PATCH] base: device profile loader, and wire hardwareConcurrency to it

The spine every spoofed surface depends on. This is the first increment: the
profile itself, plus one value carried through it end to end to prove the path.

Availability is the design constraint, not secrecy. A value read before the
profile is parsed is the host's real value, and a process that never receives
the profile reports the host's real value — both are page-observable and neither
is visible in the surface the profile was meant to fix. content_main_runner
initialises field trials before mojo, so a mojo-delivered profile cannot serve a
read that happens before mojo exists. The command line is the only channel
present at process start, and switches are not readable by web content, so
carrying it there discloses nothing to a page.

It lives in base/ because base/ is the first consumer — SysInfo — and base
cannot depend on content or chrome. That also makes it reachable from every
process type without a layering violation.

Every getter returns nullopt when the profile is absent or does not carry the
field, and callers fall through to the real value. A profile that does not
describe a surface must leave it inherited: an invented default is wrong in a
way no consumer can detect, which is worse than the host's true value.

hardwareConcurrency is patched at base::SysInfo::NumberOfProcessors rather than
at NavigatorConcurrentHardware, because thread pools size from this function.
Overriding only the Blink accessor would report the profile's core count while
the process kept running with the host's, and a worker scaling curve would
contradict the claim. Patching the source makes the claim true instead of merely
reported. The real count is split into NumberOfProcessorsImpl so the clamp can
ask for it without re-entering the override.

The claim is clamped to the host rather than refused. Claiming fewer cores than
the host has is achievable; claiming more is contradicted by measurement
whatever the function returns, and refusing to launch would trade a weak,
rarely probed signal for a hard usability failure.

Memory is parsed but not yet consumed: navigator.deviceMemory needs the value
rounded the way Blink rounds it, which is the next increment.

Known limits of this increment, recorded rather than glossed: the profile is
carried inline on the command line, which is fine for scalars and will not hold
the font lists and WebGL tables a full profile needs — that wants the shared
memory handle the field trial machinery already uses. And the switch is not yet
propagated to child processes, so this reaches the browser process only.

